{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 240,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 1868, Japan was so poor and weak that just like \nother ancient civilizations like China and Egypt,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7920,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it was staring down the barrel of Western \ncolonization. Yet, where other industrialization  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14160,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "efforts crashed and burned, Japan's 19th century \nindustrialization remained the fastest economic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20880,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "miracle in history till perhaps the rise of \nChina a century later. So, how did they do  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26160,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it? And why was Japan the only non-European nation \nto industrialize so early? The standard answer is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33280,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the so-called Meji Restoration, which was started \nby Japanese samurai that were worried that Japan  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39520,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would soon be carved up like China. You see, just \nlike British gunboats forced China to open up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45680,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trade on unequal terms, in 1854, American gunboats \nhad forced Japan to sign a couple of unequal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53360,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "treaties with Western powers, which forced them \nto cap import tariffs at just 5%. Fearing for the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60640,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very future of Japan, in 1871, Okubo Toshimichi did \nsomething unthinkable for a traditional samurai.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67760,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He cut off his samurai topnot, put on a Victorian \nthree-piece suit, and together with a core group  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74240,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of reformers, he overthrew the reigning shogun \nand replaced him with Emperor Meii. The Meiji  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80960,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "government wanted a strong military. So in \nthe 19th century, that meant steel, warships,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87040,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "guns, railroads. In other words, Japan needed \nto industrialize fast. Enrich the country,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94000,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strengthen the military. That was the job of \nOkubo Toshimichi. But that was easier said  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100720,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than done. Egypt had famously tried and failed for \ndecades to industrialize. And even the mighty Qing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107680,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dynasty's 1961 selfstrengthening movement was \nincreasingly looking like a massive failure.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114640,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yet in just 30 years time, Okubo's home ministry \nhad overseen the rise of a diversified industrial  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120720,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "base that allowed Japan to defeat a major European \npower in open war, to end the unequal treaties,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126640,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and to rather than be colonized, to become the \ncolonizer instead. But why did Okubo succeed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132960,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where so many others failed? How did he not only \nmodernize Japan but create a playbook that would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139680,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "later form the basis for the Korean, Taiwanese, \nand Chinese economic miracles in the 20th century.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145840,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Entire academic careers have been devoted to this \nvery question, and it's very easy indeed to either  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151680,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "oversimplify it or to get lost in the details. \nYet, after months of research, I think that I've  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156960,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "boiled down the essence of the Meiji miracle \nto just five foundational building blocks.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164000,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Block number one, the government foundation. Block \nnumber two, the liberal free market revolution.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170800,
    "dDurationMs": 8480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Block number three, the big government push. Block \nnumber four, the monetary creation tightrope.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179280,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Block number five, the Meji secret sauce. And as \nwe will see on their own, these building blocks  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185920,
    "dDurationMs": 9040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are not enough. Only together can they explain why \nJapan industrialized first. Okay, building block  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194960,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "number one is actually pretty uncontroversial. \nBut how far the Meiji government went in it, even  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200800,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "changing the Japanese language itself honestly \nsurprised me. It is building block number one,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206160,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the government foundation. To understand how this \nworked, consider the life story of a young boy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212240,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sakichi Toyoda, born in 1867. Every night, \nSakichi fell asleep to the rhythmic, exhausting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219920,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "clack clack clack of his mother laboring over \na primitive wooden handloom. His father was a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225600,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "humble carpenter. Had he been born in old Japan \nor across the sea in Qing, China, Sakichi's destiny  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232560,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would have been set and kind of boring. He would \nlive and die a carpenter. He was expected to be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239120,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "illiterate, poor, and quiet. Luckily, Okubo's Meiji \nRevolution shattered those rules. But to be clear,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246240,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okubo had a really good base to start from since \nliteracy rates in Japan were already higher than  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251840,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the mainland before the major restoration. Yet \nthe new government went into overdrive to push  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257840,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "them even higher. In 1872, the Maji government \nintroduced a modern westernstyled national  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 264400,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "education system. Rural populations initially \nfound these schools actually unfamiliar and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269200,
    "dDurationMs": 9680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "alien. Yet, school attendance skyrocketed from \nan estimated 45% in 1870 to over 98% by 1901. As  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278880,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a consequence, national literacy surged past 90% \nby 1910, producing a uniquely qualified workforce  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286560,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "foundation for Japan's upcoming industrialization. \nBut Okubo understood that basic literacy and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294080,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mastering complex technical engineering \nare two very different things. Therefore,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298640,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with his home ministry, he created the industrial \npromotion bureau to supercharge the imports and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305520,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "translation of western technical textbooks. The \nscale of this linguistic operation was actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311760,
    "dDurationMs": 8800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quite staggering. Recent research by economists \nHuas Shakab and Weinstein show that in 1870,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320560,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "technical books were exclusively available \nin European languages like English, France,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325120,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and German. So it didn't matter if you could read, \nyou could not read these types of books. However,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329520,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by 1910, the landscape had completely transformed. \nJapanese language technical literacy was trailing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335760,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only French and English, putting Japan on \na completely different level than any other  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341200,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Asian nation. But their research reveals that \nthis wasn't just a matter of running a routine  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347280,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "translation office. The Maji government actually \nhad to alter the very fabric of the Japanese  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353200,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "language to accommodate the industrial revolution. \nYou see, when cuttingedge technologies were  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359440,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "invented in Britain, it of course led to a wave of \nnew words in the English language like railroad,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366400,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "steam engine or telegraph. Translating these \nconcepts into related languages like French or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372640,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "German was relatively easy. However, for Japan, \nthere was a massive risk that translators would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379280,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "come up with hundreds of different translations \nfor, for example, the word telegraph. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383760,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "therefore, the Maji government massively expanded \nearlier translation efforts using standardized  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389520,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "technical dictionaries that triggered an absolute \nexplosion of new words in the Japanese language  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395280,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "after the Maji government came to power. Now the \ntiming of this all could of course not have been  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400320,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more perfect for Sakichi Toyota who even though he \nwas born poor had access to a state school where  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406960,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "he learned to read and thanks to this language \nbasis Toyota could of course read technical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413760,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "magazines and he famously read Samuel Smiles \nBritish self-help book which Toyota credited  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422000,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with setting him on the path to becoming an \ninventor. However, reading books is only half  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 427680,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the battle. To really supercharge knowledge \ncatch up to the west, Okubo's home ministry  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433360,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "subsidized Japanese students that wanted to study \nabroad. And they also brought knowledge to Japan,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439600,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "paying foreign advisers exorbitant salaries to \ntrain locals. And they organized expositions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445920,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where government imported machines and then showed \nthese to entrepreneurs. And Toyota famously built  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451440,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "his first patented wooden loom only after visiting \nthe third national industry exposition in Tokyo,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456960,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a massive event organized by the Matei government \nto explicitly showcase these machines to study by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463520,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "people like Toyota. However, when Sakichi tried \nto upgrade his famous wooden looms to steel,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469280,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "he hit a wall. In 1905, his prototype steel looms \nliterally shook themselves to pieces as they were  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476320,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "utterly humiliated in endurance tests against \nBritish looms from the legendary Plat Brothers.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482720,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Desperate, Sakichi took a massive risk. He hired \nan American master machinist, Charles Francis,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489760,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to help him out. But to pay his exorbitant \nsalary, Sakichi actually had to pay a lot of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494800,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that out of his own wage. But while he lived on \nthe edge of ruin, sacrificing his own livelihood,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501520,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "he did in fact end up learning the secrets of \nprecision steel engineering. But while technical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507040,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "knowledge allowed Toyota to become an inventor, it \nwas Okubo's aggressive infrastructure investment  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512480,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "push that allowed him to become a truly scalable \nbusiness. Toyota could of course never have made  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519360,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "metal looms without government train lines to \ntransport them to clients across the country.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524480,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And he could not have coordinated a modern supply \nchain without government constructed telegraph  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529280,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lines. And his primary customers, the textile \nmills, only operated because they could import  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535200,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ra cotton and export finished goods through modern \nstate funed boards on state subsidized ships. So  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 542800,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while there were also many smart ambitious young \npeople like Sakichi in China or Korea, they simply  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548400,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lacked the access to advanced technical \nknowledge and physical infrastructure that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 552720,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Miji government handed to Toyota. Yet if the \nstate had just built infrastructure and education,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559760,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sakiichi would actually still never have become \nJapan's greatest inventor. Which brings us to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566160,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "building block number two, the liberal free market \nrevolution. Building block two is exactly where  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 572000,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Maji reforms differed radically from the \nChineseQing reforms. You see, theQing tried  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578560,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to build Western industries with conservative \ncultural Asian values. Yet, when Okubu Toshimichi  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586160,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "traveled the world in the early 1870s to study \nwestern industry, he realized that Japan had to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592960,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "import more than just machinery. To compete with \nthe west, Japan's centuries old, rigid, feudal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599760,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cast system had to be completely dismantled. This \nsocial revolution had a massive, massive immediate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607760,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "impact on the fortunes of ordinary citizens \nlike Sakichi Toyota. under the old Tokugawa  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 614320,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "feudal structure. Sakiji as the son of a humble \ncarpenter was restricted by both rigid cast and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621360,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "guilt systems from ever really truly scaling \na disruptive manufacturing company. Therefore,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 627680,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "abolishing the class system was the first crucial \nstep to unlock Japan's human potential. However,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634640,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simply being allowed to trade was not enough to \nconvince people to risk their life savings on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640880,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unproven technologies. Therefore, in 1885, the \nMaji government passed the Patent Monopoly Act,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646960,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a legal shield that Toyota later explicitly \ncredited as the primary spark that motivated  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653760,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "him to become a professional inventor. Together, \nbuilding blocks one and two sound like a textbook  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 660160,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "triumph of classical economic 101. The government \ntook care of infrastructure and education, got rid  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665920,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of bad regulations, secure property rights, and \nsimply stepped back then to let the free market  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671760,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "work its magic. However, there is a massive \nglaring problem with this simplistic handsoff  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677840,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "narrative, and that is Argentina. You see, at this \nexact same time on the other side of the planet,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683600,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Argentina was undergoing its own jaw-dropping, \nsome would say, economic miracle. By exploiting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 691200,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vast tracks of cheap land and flooding the market \nwith agricultural exports, Argentina became almost  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697440,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as rich as Great Britain on paper. However, \ndespite looking like the rise of Japan at first,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703760,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Japan actually industrialized, which later allowed \nJapan to truly join the rich world while Argentina  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710320,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was left behind. This puzzling divergence is \nwhat famously led the Nobel laurate economist  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 716960,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Simon Knet declare that there are four kinds of \ncountries in the world. Developed, underdeveloped,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722960,
    "dDurationMs": 8480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Japan and Argentina. So why did 19th century \nJapan successfully industrialized while 19th  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731440,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "century free marketloving Argentina failed at \nindustrialization. This brings us to building  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738800,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "block number three, the big push. You see in \ndevelopment economics the big push theory was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744480,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first proposed by economist Paul Rosenstein Rodan \nin 1943 and he stated that industries in poor  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751280,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nations suffer from a coordination problem and \nthat to solve it a country must launch a massive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757040,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "coordinated investment push across multiple \ndependent industries all at once. To see this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763920,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in practice let's look at Sakichi's Toyota's steel \nloom factory. To build these advanced steel looms,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 771440,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sakichi needed a steady supply of highquality \ncoal and iron. However, importing these heavy raw  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778560,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "materials from Europe was prohibitively expensive. \nAnd therefore, Japan desperately needed its own  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784720,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "domestic mining industry. But who on earth would \nrisk a life savings opening a coal mine when there  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791440,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were no modern factories to buy all of that \ncoal? Should Toyota wait to build a factory  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 798080,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "until the mines existed? Or should the mines \nonly exist if Toyota's factories were already  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803840,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "running? The answer is simply that without some \nintervention, neither of these would get built,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 810560,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at least not quick enough. And this is why Akubo's \nhome affair department stepped in to play the role  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816800,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the ultimate grand coordinator under a national \npolicy called promoting production and encouraging  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 823440,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "industry. They did not just write checks. They \nactually built model factories like the Shinmachi  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 831840,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spinning mill to show private entrepreneurs how \nmodern manufacturing was supposedly done. And for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838000,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "heavy industry, they went even further directly \nconstructing state-of-the-art installations like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843360,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Nagasaki shipyard and the massive Miki \ncoal mine. Sakichi could only make advanced  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 850480,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "steel looms because Okubo had given the textile \nand mining industries that Sakichi depended on a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858240,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "big push. However, as state planners all over \nthe world would discover in the 20th century  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864320,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "under communism, coordinating an entire national \neconomy by just a government is incredibly hard.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871040,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And indeed, Okubo's state-run enterprises quickly \nbecame notorious money fits plagued by massive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 878160,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "operational inefficiencies. And to make matters \nworse, Toshimichi faced a violent backlash at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884800,
    "dDurationMs": 9440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "home. In 1877, his former friend Saigo Takamorei \nled the southern samurai in the Satsuma Rebellion,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894240,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "arguing that modernization was just moving far \ntoo fast in Japan. And although the government  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899760,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eventually crushed the rebellion, the financial \ncost was so massive that it forced the Maji  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904880,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "government to turn to the printing presses, \nsending the Japanese economy into a spiral of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 910720,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hyperinflation. And to make matters worse, while \nhis side would prevail, the Satsuma rebellion's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 917200,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fallout would prematurely end Okubo's role in \nthe maji economic miracle. In 1878, while riding  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 924480,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "his carriage, he was ambushed and assassinated by \nconservative samurai men who felt betrayed by his  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 930880,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "modern world. When they searched his body, they \nthen found that he was actually carrying a letter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935680,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from his old friend, third turd rebel rival, Saigo \nTakamorei. Okubu had sacrificed his friendships,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 943200,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "his culture, and finally his life to build the \nfoundations of modern Japan. Yet he died nearly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 949280,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "broke, having poured even much of his personal \nfunds into now failing state enterprises. So with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 956080,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the country on the brink of financial collapse, \nthe government turned to a man who could not  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 961120,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be more different. Okubu's former disciple, \nMatsucata Masayoshi. Unlike his charismatic,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968880,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "visionary mentor, Matsucata was a numbers driven, \nquiet, gentle man. Yet, as it would turn out,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 976240,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a quiet calculating numbers man was precisely what \nJapan needed. You see, to kill hyperinflation,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 982640,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Matsucata presented himself as a brutal fiscal \nhawk. He slashed government spending, sucked  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 989280,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cash out of circulation and let interest rates \nskyrocket. Millions of poor farmers went bankrupt,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 995680,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Matsucata became the most hated man in Japan. \nHowever, a closer look by historian Steven J.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1002400,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ericson actually reveals Matsugatu didn't just \nblindly cut budgets all across the board. Instead,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1009440,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "he targeted only the state's worst inefficiencies. \nAnd therefore, Erikson called his policy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1016000,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expansionary austerity, not full-blown austerity. \nAnd perhaps even more importantly, Matsucata  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1022720,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "realized that while the state-led big push had \nsucceeded in building the physical infrastructure,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1027680,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the government was simply terrible at running \nday-to-day businesses. and therefore he engineered  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1032880,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a brilliant pivot. Rather than liquidating these \nfailing state-owned enterprises, Matsucata sold  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1040080,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "them off at a fire sale to wealthy, well-connected \nmerchant families. And these families bundled the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1047520,
    "dDurationMs": 8480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "assets into highly diversified mega corporations \nknown as Zybatsu like Mishubi, Mitsui, and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1056000,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sumitomo. To sweeten the deal, the government even \ngranted these Zybatsu mega corporations, private  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1063040,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "banking licenses, and lucrative monopolies over \nstate shipping routes and mines. But crucially,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1070160,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this did not mean that the Mei state left industry \ncompletely to the free market. No, they remained  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1077040,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "heavily involved in high-risk infrastructure \nprojects and heavy industries like Yawata steel  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1082800,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "works. And crucially, to avoid falling prey to \nthe type of crony capitalism that ruins almost  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1089360,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every developing country it touches, Matsucata let \nthe Zibatu compete amongst each other ruthlessly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1097040,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to ensure efficiency. Just as importantly, these \nlucrative monopolies on mines and transport routes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1103680,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were strictly conditional on performance. If \na Zibbatu failed to run a mine productively,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1110400,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then the state would simply take it away \nand hand it to a competitor. Therefore,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1114960,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as economists Randall Mor and Masau Nakamura \nargue, the Zibbatsu were not a parasitic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1122880,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "oligarchy. They were simply a more efficient \nsolution to give Japanese industries the big  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1128160,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "push that they needed to power Japan's shocking \nmilitary victories in the first SinoJapanese war  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1134240,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in 1895 and the Russo European power Japanese \nwar in 1905 which Japan won. And by defeating a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1141920,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "major European superpower like Russia, Japan had \nreally proved that it could no longer be treated  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1146720,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a secondass nation which ended the unequal \ntreaties. And while he was not there to see it,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1153920,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okubo's original mission, enrich the \ncountry, strengthen the military,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1158080,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was now officially complete. However, 1905 was \nnot the end of the road for Sakei Toyota. In fact,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1165840,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "his ultimate success would rely directly on \nthis unique Zibatu system. You see, when China's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1173120,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Boxer Rebellion broke out, Toyota's textile \nexports to China crashed to practically zero,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1178800,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "threatening to bankrupt his young firm. And in a \npure free market, his company would have surely  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1184960,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "died right then and there. However, because Toyota \nhad the deep pocketed Mitsui Zibbatsu backing him.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1192720,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, he could tank the losses and restructure \nToyota's business. And this meant that in 1929,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1199040,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "he served the ultimate poetic justice to the \nBritish Plat brothers, whose superior machines  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1204640,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "embarrassed Toyota decades earlier. They admitted \ndefeat. They bought the patent rights to Toyota's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1211120,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "revolutionary type G automatic loom for £100,000. \nThe student had officially become the master,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1218800,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but Sakichi was dying. On his deathbed in 1930, \nhe looked at his son Picho and he did not tell  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1226400,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "him to keep building looms. Instead, it said that \nhe handed him the massive British payout and gave  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1232480,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "him one final challenge. I did my part with \ntextiles. Now you use this money to build the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1239200,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Japanese automobile. Ultimately it was roughly \naround this time that Argentina truly stagnated  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1246720,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as an agrarian nation and also turned to more \nstate involvement looking at Japan trying to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1253200,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "industrialize. But it became clear that Japan had \nprovided the blueprint for the world when it comes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1260160,
    "dDurationMs": 8800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to a state and mega corporation guided big push. \nSo, this is where our story could end. But for me,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1268960,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it was not enough. I still had two nagging \nquestions that standard economic theory, even with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1274560,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the big push building block, struggles to answer. \nNumber one, how did a dirt poor country like Japan  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1281120,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually pay for all of this without drowning in \nforeign debt? And second, sure, Japan's government  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1287200,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "had increased education and infrastructure, but \ndidn't established Western competitors enjoy these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1292880,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "advantages already? How did Japanese startups like \nToyota actually out compete established Western  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1299120,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "giants who already had a decadesl long head \nstart? To answer these two nagging questions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1306800,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we have to look at our next building blocks. \nStarting with building block number four,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1313040,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dual monetary manipulation. Okay. To understand \nhow the initially poor Miji government financed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1319120,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its economic miracle, we have to understand \nthe dual nature of money creation. First,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1326240,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there is the type of money that the state cannot \ncreate. Foreign money like British pounds, gold or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1333040,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "silver at the time. This was arguably the most \nimportant type of money for an industrializing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1339120,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "state like Japan because it needed it to import \nadvanced western machinery and to hire very  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1345520,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expensive foreign technical experts. At the time, \nstandard economics prescribed that a developing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1351920,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "state should borrow foreign money, of course, to \nindustrialize. Then when the industrialization was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1358400,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "complete, the state could repay these loans thanks \nto export revenues in gold, pounds, and silver.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1365760,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, the fate of Egypt served as Okubo's and \nMatsucata's ultimate horror scenario. You see,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1373040,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Egypt had borrowed to the hilt to fund a state-led \nbig push industrialization effort. However,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1379040,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Egypt's ability to pay back those loans \ndepended entirely on exporting cotton.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1384000,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And when the American Civil War ended and cheap \nAmerican cotton flooded back onto global markets,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1389840,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Egyptian cotton prices crashed and Egypt could \nno longer service its debts. Next, Western  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1395600,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "powers used those unpaid debts as a convenient \npretext to militarily invade and colonize Egypt,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1402800,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the exact fate the Maji samurai were trying to \nprevent. So instead of borrowing, Okubo and later  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1409120,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Matakata tried their best to fund every single \nforeign import using the cash generated from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1415520,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "domestic exports. However, this left a major \npuzzle. How do you jumpstart domestic export  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1422000,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "industries without foreign money from foreign \nborrowing for these machines and knowledge?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1428080,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is where domestic money creation comes in. \nIn a sovereign government, they can essentially  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1433440,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "print this out of thin air. This only becomes \ninflationary if it's not backed by a corresponding  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1440080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "increase in real economic production. Therefore, \nif you print money to build a productive factory,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1445600,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you can theoretically increase both the \nsupply of money and the supply of goods,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1449760,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "keeping prices stable. However, it could take \nyears for factories to become operational. So,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1456160,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to prevent inflation, Okubo turned to taxation. \nUnder the old feudal shogunut, taxes were paid in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1463280,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physical rice. However, this meant that government \nrevenue fluctuated wildly depending on the weather  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1469200,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and crop yields, making long-term planning and \ninvestment in factories impossible. Therefore,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1474000,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okubu introduced a radical 3% land tax based \nstrictly on the monetary value of the land to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1480000,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be paid exclusively in cash. Not only did this \nstabilized state revenue needed for the big push  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1485680,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by changing from rice to currency, it greatly \nincentivized ordinary Japanese people to turn to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1492320,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "open bank accounts. And this allowed statebacked \nbanks and private Zibatu conglomerates to further  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1498960,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "increase the money supply through private bank \ncredit creation to fund upcoming industries. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1505280,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to prevent unproductive use of credit, the state \nand Zibatu banks had highly targeted productive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1512320,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mandates. State banks lend money strictly \nfor agricultural development, for example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1517040,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while Zibatu banks funded mines, shipping lines, \nand heavy manufacturing. This was not yet used  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1522880,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for stock market speculation or housing bubbles \nlike we may see today. And because nearly every  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1527760,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "newly created yen went directly into expanding \nproductive capacity, the supply of goods roughly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1533280,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "kept pace with the supply of money, keeping \ninflation firmly in check. However, this very  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1538080,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "success did greatly threaten Japan's access to \nforeign money. You see, the domestic money supply  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1544160,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "grew and Japan's citizens got richer. Of course, \nthen they wanted to buy consumer goods. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1550480,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because Japan's own industries were still in their \ninfancy, Western factories could produce goods  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1555280,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "far more efficiently and cheaply than Japanese \nstartups. Therefore, citizens naturally wanted  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1560720,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to use their new money to buy superior western \nimports for which they needed pound, silver,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1566080,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or gold. That the state and industry of course \nneeded to import machines and knowledge. That's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1571840,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a problem. Therefore, historically, developing \nnations like the United States tended to protect  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1576960,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their young infant industries from foreign \ncompetition behind giant tariff walls, decreasing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1583840,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "imports. However, remember those unequal treaties \nwithout the ability to raise tariffs. There was a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1590320,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very real risk that Japan's advanced industries \nwould be strangled in their cradle by cheap  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1595280,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Western imports, regardless of how much capital \nthe state banks and zibatu poured into them. So,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1601760,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how did Japan manage to protect its domestic \nindustries and conserve precious silver and gold  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1607440,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when it was legally forbidden to use tariffs? This \nbrings us to the final crucial ingredient of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1614240,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Maji miracle, the Magi secret sauce, which I think \nyou could summarize as stealthily suppressing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1621920,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "imports and secretly encouraging exports. So, how \ndid the Maji Samurai do this? Well, I think there  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1629040,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are four main strategies that we actually still \noften see today. The first is export subsidies.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1635600,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Under Okubu, this was pretty direct as he \ngave, for example, just money to Japanese  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1640400,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shipping companies, helping exports. But also, the \nseemingly orthodox Matsucatu started the practice  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1647680,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of subsidizing exports, supporting banks via the \nnewly created central bank. Even Masugatus's fire  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1654320,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sales of state factories to zibatus at absolute \nbottom of the barrel prices. Well, you could look  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1660000,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at this and say, well, that's clearly a failure of \nthe state. They're giving away money for free. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1666240,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you could also say it was actually a masterclass \nin d-risking. You see, by building these factories  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1671040,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first, the state essentially absorbed the risk \n100%. So then when the Zabatu took them over,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1678000,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they started on a much closer footing to western \nconglomerates which had had factories for decades.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1683760,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Second, Japan gave rising industries a massive \nboost simply by only procuring Japanese companies  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1689920,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for state projects such as railroads or \nnavy ships. This massively predictable  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1695440,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "state demand meant that affected Japanese heavy \nindustries essentially didn't have to worry about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1700960,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "foreign competition for years. Third, and perhaps \nentirely by accident, Matsugatu premiered a staple  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1707200,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of Asian development miracles, undervaluing your \ncurrency when he chose to keep Japan on the silver  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1714080,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "standard when most of the Western world shifted \ncollectively towards the gold standard. You see,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1720160,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because this massive global shift meant that the \nvalue of silver collapsed to gold, it also meant  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1726480,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the value of the yen, which remained tight \nto silver automatically depreciated against the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1731840,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "US dollar and the British pound. And as a result, \nJapanese products became much cheaper compared  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1737920,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the West even though tariffs were low. This \nsimultaneously helped reduce imports and boosted  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1744720,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exports, making more international money available \nfor importing machines and knowledge. But perhaps  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1750000,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the darkest part of the Japanese development \nmiracle was that while it greatly improved  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1754880,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the life of poor, highly talented people like \nSakichi, Japanese industry was also built on a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1761760,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark foundational reality. The brutal exploitation \nof the Japanese workforce. You see, while the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1768560,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "West introduced labor protection laws, Japanese \nworkers enjoyed none of these. Japanese factories  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1774560,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "routinely ran grueling 14 to 16-hour shifts \nand heavily exploited young female peasants,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1780880,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "contract laborers who were housed in restrictive \nfactory dormitories. Meanwhile, the Maji state  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1787520,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "suppressed labor unions, outlawing strikes, and \nviewed organized labor as a dangerous socialist  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1794000,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "threat. Again, this benefited both exports \nby allowing Japanese startups to consistently  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1800800,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "undercut Western competition on price because \nthey had to pay lower wages. And simultaneously,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1807040,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these low wages kept imports low, allowing the \nMaji government to preserve its precious gold and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1813360,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "foreign reserves and completely avoid the Egyptian \ndebt trap. Yet, it was this very strategy that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1820560,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "arguably caused Japan to fall into its own trap \nand become a fascist military dictatorship after  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1826720,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Great Depression plunged large parts of the \nalready poor export oriented Japanese workforce  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1833600,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into poverty. It was under these conditions \nthat rogue elements of the Japanese army started  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1838960,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "violently targeting Japan's wealthy elites like \nthe director general of the Mitsui Zibbatsu. As  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1846160,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the public supported the violence given their \nterrible circumstances, this arguably set the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1852480,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scene for the civilian government to completely \nsurrender eventually to the powerful military,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1856880,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which later plunged Japan into World War II, which \nof course ultimately destroyed its industries. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1864560,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that's the story for another time. For now, I hope \nyou found this insightful, this deep dive into the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1870080,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "story of Japanese legends like Okubu, Matsugatu, \nand Sakichi Toyota. I hope it was insightful to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1877440,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "see how the Maji miracle can be explained by a \ncombination of standard free market economics,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1882400,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sure, but also big government and zybatsuleled \npushes and a set of forbidden techniques,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1890320,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "many of which you can still clearly see today \nin major inspired economic miracles like those  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1894800,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of Taiwan, Korea, and China. This was my modern \ninterpretation of this story. But what if you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1901680,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "had lived in the late 19th century and early \n20th century when this all happened? Well,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1906800,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in that case, you could read all about the events \nof the Maji restoration and the rise of Japan if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1912160,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you consistently read our advertising sponsor, \nThe Economist. And even today, if you don't want  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1917440,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to miss the next development miracle, I highly \nrecommend you check out their coverage on the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1922240,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ascent of India's economy, Vietnam's fast but \nfragile rise, and finally their analysis on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1928400,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how East Asia should respond to the rapid rise \nof Chinese industry. As these analysis show,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1935200,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Economist delivers insights that truly help \nyou to see the bigger picture. And this is why  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1940800,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I highly recommend that you subscribe to The \nEconomist, which now offers an exclusive 35%  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1947360,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "discount for yes, money macro viewers. Whether you \nprefer their daily journalism in the Economist app  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1954640,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or you are like me and really enjoy catching \nup on the global economy over the weekend with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1959600,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a nice cup of coffee and the print edition. \nYou'll always stay on top of the latest global  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1964960,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "developments and of course support journalism \nthat values factual information, integrity,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1970400,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and an independent point of view. So do not miss \nout. Click the link in description or top comment  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1977600,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "below or head over to economist.com/moneymicro \nto claim your exclusive 35% discount"
    } ]
  } ]
}
